editorial_contributions.text:
Let \(G\) be a simple graph without isolated vertices and let \(v\in V(G)\). Vertex \(v\) dominates itself and all its neighbors. A set \(D\) is a double dominating set if every vertex of \(G\) is dominated by at least two vertices of \(D\). Two disjoint sets \(X,Y\subset V(G)\) form a double coalition of \(G\) if they are not double dominating sets of \(G\) but \(X\cup Y\) is a double dominating set of \(G\). A partition \(\{V_1,\dots,V_k\}\) of \(V(G)\) is a double coalition partition if every set \(V_i\), \(i\in[k]\), forms a double coalition with some set \(V_j\), \(j\in[k]\), \(i\neq j\). The maximum number of sets in a double coalition partition of \(G\) is called the double coalition number and is denoted by \(\operatorname{DC}(G)\).

The degree of \(v\) is the number of its neighbors in \(G\) and is denoted by \(d(v)\). If every vertex of \(G\) has the same degree \(d(v)=r\), then \(G\) is an \(r\)-regular graph. The class of all \(r\)-regular graphs is denoted by \(\mathcal{G}_r^{\mathrm{reg}}\). By \(\mathcal{G}_r\) is denoted the class of graphs where \(d(v)\geq r\) for every vertex \(v\in V(G)\). The authors consider the following two constants \(\theta_r^{\mathrm{reg}}\) and \(\theta_r\): 
\[
\theta_r^{\mathrm{reg}}=\sup_{G\in \mathcal{G}_r^{\mathrm{reg}}}\frac{\operatorname{DC}(G)}{r}\; \text{ and }\; \theta_r=\sup_{G\in \mathcal{G}_r}\frac{\operatorname{DC}(G)}{r}.
\]
In other words, the mentioned constants yield upper bounds \(\operatorname{DC}(G)\leq r\cdot\theta_r^{\mathrm{reg}}\) for any \(G\in\mathcal{G}_r^{\mathrm{reg}}\) and \(\operatorname{DC}(G)\leq r\cdot\theta_r\) for any \(G\in\mathcal{G}_r\).

The main results are the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item \(\theta_r^{\mathrm{reg}}\geq 2\cdot\frac{r-1}{r}\) for every \(r\geq 3\);
\item the above lower bound is sharp for \(r\in\{3,4\}\);
\item \(\theta_r\geq 2\) for every \(r\geq 3\);
\item the above lower bound is sharp for \(r=3\);
\item \(\theta_r\geq 3\) for sufficiently large \(r\).
\end{itemize}
